  "primer": {
    "text": "The United States has kept forces in Iraq for more than two decades, and Iraq is the country where that presence has been based. A withdrawal is the formal end of that long presence through a drawdown — the planned removal of troops and the closing out of the deployment. That presence has been part of the region's security arrangement, so how it ends matters beyond the two countries. Changes to who provides security inside Iraq carry implications for neighboring states and for broader regional security.",
    "built_at": "2026-10-04T07:26:06.472952+00:00"
  },
  "current": {
    "date": "2026-10-03",
    "bottom_line": "The September 30 completion of the 23-year US presence remains the defining fact; October 1-3 was quiet with no new verified clashes, ISIS attacks, or replacement security arrangements. Confidence is limited to reported headlines — one Reuters-noted withdrawal completion on October 1 — with no new researched confirmation in this window.",
